A||||6||Fierochic88||Drop Spindles vs. Lowering Springs||||1||
Z||000000||Fierochic88||12-14-2001||07:43 PM||||The suspension uneducated has a question  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I am getting ready to start on my 85 GT and will need to buy tires for it once the engine project is completed (the last owner balled the tires that are on the car).  I currently have 16 in. rims but have yet to decide what profile tires I will be buying.<P>My question is, what is the difference between drop spindles and lowering springs?  I would eventually like to SLIGHTLY lower the car and want to take this into account when buying the tires.  I don't want that much gap once the car is actually lowered, but I need to buy the tires soon.<P>Also, drop spindles are only made for the front?  Is that true?  Can I have drop spindles in the front and lowering springs in the back?<P>Thanks for all your help.<P>Jennifer<P>------------------<BR>IM Username:  FieroChic88<BR>Silver 1988 GT<BR>Silver 1986 2m4<BR>Cobalt Blue 85 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.personal.psu.edu/users/j/j/jjr161/Fierochic88Signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jncomutt||12-14-2001||07:46 PM||||Well I can inform ya on as much as I know, and let the good guys add what they will later.<P>Drop spindles are much more expensive than the springs.  They will however keep the suspension geometry the same.  The spindles are also only available for the front, and yes you can use spindles up front, and drop springs in the rear.<P>You might not however, want to use both upfront.  I believe it was ?Chester? (dont' recall) who did this and said it was too low.<P>Hope it helps some<p>[This message has been edited by Jncomutt (edited 12-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||12-14-2001||09:16 PM||||In principle, drop spindles should work better because they lower the car without reducing suspension travel or corrupting suspension geomtry.  <B>However</B>, '84-'87 Fiero front suspension geometry is pretty poor already; lowering springs may actually improve it!<P>I lowered my '85 SE moderately by cutting the springs.  (Yes I know it's crude, but it works.)  So far, reduced travel has not been a problem.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||qwikgta||12-14-2001||09:42 PM||||And you may want to consider the coilover kit idea for the rear too, you can get the $260 kit from Heldmotorsports that replaces your rear spring/spring pad and will allow you to adjust your ride height to whatever you want, also allows wider rear wheels.  I believe that Chris West also has this kit for about the same price.  This kit requires you to use your struts or Koni/KYB replacements and you cut off the spring pad and replace it with a threaded sleeve.  Visit the web site and it explains it better than I do.<P>Rob<P>BTW...I just cut my coils to achive a 2" drop.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sig_Pic_copy.jpg">  <BR>"DRIVE IT LIKE YOU STOLE IT"||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tigger||12-14-2001||09:54 PM||||I have Street Dreams 2" drop spindles up front and 1-1/2" suspension techniques springs in the rear.  You can install a coil over conversions for the rear.  See the Held Motorsports group purchase thread.  I think Held also makes rear drop spindles but they are for 88's only.<P>Anyway, I've been driving mine for about a year with them and I'm very happy.  2" drop is somewhat drastic, you have to watch for things in the road and take drives at angles but other than being cautious it's not too bad.  Take a look at the "current project" link on my site, I've got some pictures of the spindles.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerwinter3.gif">           <BR>T-T-F-N, Ta Ta For Now!<BR><A HREF="http://www.envy.nu/fiero/" TARGET=_blank>Tigger's Fiero Page</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||artherd||12-14-2001||11:28 PM||||Hi Jen!<P>This for the blue car with the Momo Sports, right?  (man that car is pretty!)<P>When you lower a car's ride height, you want to bump up it's spring rate to a matching degree, to avoid the new suspension from going through your fenders! :P<P>When you get drop spindles (the best way to go, especially on earlier cars which don't have a lot of alignment lattitude) you'll want to get a set of *SLIGHTLY* stiffer STOCK height springs, and new good shocks too.<P>In the rear, Dave Held's coilover strut, or coilover adapter kit (you weld the latter onto your current strut) will give you *Unlimited* selections of springs at $10ea, and total ride-height control  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>PS: instead of the spindles, if you just want a 1" drop or so in front, you can go with lowering springs.  Won't be quite as nice, but will be a LOT cheaper.  I would NOT go lower than 1.5" drop, and better do 1" even with just springs on an 84-87 car.<P>Hope this helps you out!  <P>One more thing, invest in a $99 set of Earls SS braided brake lines, they're solid Teflon with stainless outer braid, and will NEVER explode, unlike old stock lines!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000006||theogre||12-14-2001||11:48 PM||||You're driving on those cowpaths PA tries to pass off as roads around Reading and you want to lower the car? Are you nuts? I've driven plenty in your area. Just an inch or so will likely mean beating the hell out of the bottom of the car, or way worse, slamming the suspension and breaking a ball joint.<P>I don't know what engine you are putting in.... That's a factor. Depending on tire size, you may need to recalibrate the speed sensor not only for the dash but for the ECM as well.<P>Before you think about lowering.....<P>get new gas shocks/struts with velocity sensitive valves. Gabreil's VST is one example. Most good modern shocks/struts have such valves. This type valve stiffens the car considerably when you hit a big bump but it still rides decent the rest of the time. I was able to get top end Gabriel, carry out, from Pep Boys for less than $150. I'm not sure if I hit a sale or what. <P>Next, install polyurethane or solid cradle bushings. This will keep the cradle from dancing all over. You can get poly at good prices from <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com/" TARGET=_blank>Suspension Restoration Parts Co.</A><P>Then get a good alignment.<P>Tires are fun... I could write a book just on tires.... We have a link here someplace to a tire size calculator that would tell you how much differant 2 sizes are. I went and found it again... <A HREF="http://www.secret-secret.com/turbo/DIY/tirecalc/turbotire.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.secret-secret.com/turbo/DIY/tirecalc/turbotire.html</A> <P>If you must lower it, redo the entire suspension. New bushings, ball joints, bumpers for A arm and struts, etc. PennDOT is worse than DelDOT and NYDOT combined. The County roads are even worse. I'm amazed any of the vehicles I drove up there lasted thru it. More than once I've had to get cars suspensions aligned or repaired after drives in PA. If you don't have everything at 100% something will break. likely soon. even at 100% the chances you'll slam the suspension and break something are still not good.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
